= Framing effect — Technical =

== Invariance ==
A strict rational-choice invariance principle requires preference to remain stable across extensionally equivalent descriptions.

Framing effects violate this principle when representation changes preference without changing outcomes.

== Prospect-theoretic account ==
Prospect theory evaluates outcomes relative to a reference point:

<pre>
value(x) = gains/losses relative to reference
</pre>

Losses typically receive steeper weighting than comparable gains, and probability weighting can interact with the frame.

== Multiple framing families ==
Risky-choice, attribute, and goal framing should not be treated as one identical mechanism.

They differ in task structure, dependent variable, and proposed process.

== Pragmatics ==
Equivalent truth conditions do not guarantee identical conversational meaning.

A frame may signal contrast, norm, goal, or speaker intention. Strong claims of irrationality therefore require careful stimulus design.

